Q: JSON Serializing java.sql.Time, java.sql.Date, and java.sql.Timestamp with minimal allocation A: <p>You can drop both <code>GregorianCalendar</code>s and get true zero-allocation extraction with pure integer math, the same epoch-day algorithm <code>java.time.LocalDate</code> uses internally (<a href="http://howardhinnant.github.io/date_algorithms.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Hinnant's <code>civil_from_days</code></a>):</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>private static void civilFromEpochDay(long epochDay, int[] ymd) {
    long z = epochDay + 719_468L;
    long era = Math.floorDiv(z, 146_097L);
    long doe = z - era * 146_097L;
    long yoe = (doe - doe/1460 + doe/36524 - doe/146096) / 365;
    long y   = yoe + era * 400;
    long doy = doe - (365*yoe + yoe/4 - yoe/100);
    long mp  = (5*doy + 2) / 153;
    ymd[2] = (int) (doy - (153*mp + 2)/5 + 1);          // day
    ymd[1] = (int) (mp + (mp &lt; 10 ? 3 : -9));           // month
    ymd[0] = (int) (y + (ymd[1] &lt;= 2 ? 1 : 0));         // year
}
</code></pre>
<p>Time-of-day is trivial integer division once you have ms-of-day (<code>s = msOfDay/1000, hour = s/3600, min = (s/60)%60, sec = s%60</code>).</p>
<p>Use <code>Math.floorDiv/floorMod</code> (not <code>//%</code>) to get <code>epochDay/millisOfDay</code> from <code>getTime()</code>, so pre-1970 dates still work.</p>
<p>For <code>Time</code>'s local-zone offset: <code>TimeZone.getDefault()</code> clones on every call, cache it once as a field, same as your calendars:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>private final TimeZone localZone = TimeZone.getDefault(); // fetch ONCE
</code></pre>
<p><code>localZone.getOffset(millis)</code> itself returns a primitive <code>int</code> with no allocation.
Add that offset to the raw millis before computing millis-of-day for <code>Time</code>, and you've eliminated the <code>Calendar</code>s entirely.</p>

Q: Word Games in Javascript A: <p>You can do this with a <strong>weighted random selection</strong>. Instead of giving every letter the same chance, give each letter a weight based on its frequency.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre><code>const frequencies = {
  E: 11.1607, A: 8.4966, R: 7.5809, I: 7.5448,
  O: 7.1635, T: 6.9509, N: 6.6544, S: 5.7351,
  L: 5.4893, C: 4.5388, U: 3.6308, D: 3.3844,
  P: 3.1671, M: 3.0129, H: 3.0034, G: 2.4705,
  B: 2.0720, F: 1.8121, Y: 1.7779, W: 1.2899,
  K: 1.1016, V: 1.0074, X: 0.2902, Z: 0.2722,
  J: 0.1965, Q: 0.1962
};

const letters = Object.keys(frequencies);
const total = Object.values(frequencies)
  .reduce((sum, value) =&gt; sum + value, 0);

function randomLetter() {
    let random = Math.random() * total;

    for (const letter of letters) {
        random -= frequencies[letter];

        if (random &lt; 0) {
            return letter;
        }
    }
}

function generateLetters(count) {
    return Array.from({ length: count }, randomLetter);
}

console.log(generateLetters(100).join(&quot;&quot;));
</code></pre>
<p>The important thing is that <strong>11.1607 doesn't mean you will get exactly 11 E's every time</strong>.</p>
<p>It means that on each draw, E has roughly an 11.16% chance of being selected. So one run of <a href="https://www.5letterwordfindertool.com/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">5 letters  words </a>might contain 9 E's, another might contain 14, and so on. If you generate a much larger number of letters, the percentages should get closer to the values you're using.</p>
<p>If you actually need exactly 11 E's, 8 A's, etc. in every batch of 100, I would use a different approach: calculate the required count for each letter, put those letters into an array, and then shuffle the array using Fisher-Yates.</p>
<p>For a word game, though, the weighted random approach is probably the simpler option.</p>

Q: Spring web socket messaging - subscribe and send initial message A: <p>I think I found a solution for your problem:</p>
<p>You have to subscribe to the user specific topic.
In my example I've created a topic <code>/topic/progress</code>.</p>
<p>I subscribe to <code>/user/topic/progress</code></p>
<pre><code>stompClient.subscribe('/user/topic/progress', progressMessage =&gt; {
      ...
})
</code></pre>
<p>I've created a component that listens for the <code>SessionSubscribeEvent</code> in order to react on new subscriptions:</p>
<pre><code>@Component
public class WebSocketEventListener {

    @Autowired
    private WebSocketService webSocketService;

    @Autowired
    private ProgressService progressService;

    @EventListener
    public void handleWebSocketConnectListener(SessionSubscribeEvent event) throws IllegalAccessException {
        if(Objects.equals(event.getMessage().getHeaders().get(&quot;simpDestination&quot;), &quot;/user/topic/progress&quot;)) {
            webSocketService.sendCurrentProgessToUser(progressService.getProgress(), event.getUser().getName());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The WebSocketservice is used to send the messages to the subscribing users.
I have a method to broadcast stuff to a topic and one to send it to specific users only. The broadcast method isn't the original one. I use the <code>SimpUserRegistry</code> to get all subscribers and send the message to each separately:</p>
<pre><code>@Controller
@Service
public class WebSocketService {

    @Autowired
    private SimpMessagingTemplate simpMessagingTemplate;

    @Autowired
    private SimpUserRegistry simpUserRegistry;

    private static final String WS_PROGRESS_DESTINATION = &quot;/topic/progress&quot;;

    public void broadcastCurrentProgress(Progress progress) {
        ProgressDto progressDto = new ProgressDto(progress);
        List&lt;String&gt; subscribers = simpUserRegistry.getUsers().stream()
                .map(SimpUser::getName).collect(Collectors.toList());
        for(String username : subscribers) {
            simpMessagingTemplate.convertAndSendToUser(username, WS_PROGRESS_DESTINATION, progressDto);
        }
    }

    public void sendCurrentProgessToUser(Progress progress, String name) {
        ProgressDto progressDto = new ProgressDto(progress);
        simpMessagingTemplate.convertAndSendToUser(name, WS_PROGRESS_DESTINATION, progressDto);
    }
}
</code></pre>
